  "worked_solution": "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =(b-2-1)^{2}+{(2b-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={b}^{2}-6b+9+4{b}^{2}+4b+1 \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-2b+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{\u2032}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(b) & =10b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{\u2032}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-2 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{\u2033}(\\frac{1}{5})=10>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{1}{5}.$",
